# No end-of-line conversion, anywhere.
#
# Git on Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true and rewrites LF to CRLF on
# checkout. That is invisible until something hashes a file, and this
# repository hashes several:
#
#   * about.toml pins a SHA-256 of LICENSE for every first-party crate's
#     clarification. Converted to CRLF the file hashes
#     1eb85fc97224598dad1852b5d6483bbcf0aa8608790dcc657a5a2a761ae9c8c6
#     instead of c71d239df91726fc519c6eb72d318ec65820627232b2f796219e87dcf35d0ab4,
#     cargo-about rejects the clarification, and the build dies with
#     "no `license` specified, and no license files were found" — a message
#     that says nothing about line endings. This cost a CI run on 2026-08-15.
#   * The macOS installer verifies a compiled-in SHA-256 of the script it
#     elevates (service.rs), and install-daemon.sh hashes the daemon it stages.
#
# `-text` on everything is deliberately blunt: an allowlist would have to be
# extended every time a new hashed file appears, and the failure mode of
# forgetting is a corrupted artifact rather than a compile error. Nothing here
# needs CRLF — PowerShell reads LF scripts, and the NSIS sources are ASCII.
#
# Note this only governs what git writes to the working tree. Existing clones
# keep whatever they already have until a file changes; a fresh checkout (which
# is what CI does every time) gets byte-exact content immediately.
* -text
